  abstract={NeRPIM, a world-first energy-efficient 3D neural rendering processing-in-memory (PIM) processor, is proposed for mobile devices. A PIM-based renderer reduces the per-sample rendering energy with 8T SRAM PIM. Space encoding blockwise mapping reduces the global memory access and reduces overall rendering energy. A ray-wise dynamic block reuse maximizes the reusability of PIM with enhanced throughput. NeRPIM is fabricated in 28nm CMOS technology and occupies 5.1 mm2 of die area. As a result, the processor only consumes 129.8 mW of power at 30.7 FPS (NeRF synthetic dataset), achieving a state-of-the-art energy efficiency of 4.2 mJ/frame.},
  keywords={Three-dimensional displays;Random access memory;Very large scale integration;Rendering (computer graphics);Throughput;CMOS technology;Energy efficiency;Metaverse;neural rendering;processing in memory;SRAM;and energy-efficiency},
